    "media:imagetext": "'ney deserve a better fate. We got here just at dusk, a lot of large\nampans came out, and all the refugees\nand their baggage were doad\ned then and they were rowed ashore in the dark. I looked down on tham\nbeing loaded enë from\nthe upper deck. One woman had a little boy I\nguess, about nine or ten months old. He was leaning over, out of her\narms, very much interested in the top of a box about level with his\nkeed. chin. He caught hold of the rope and pulled and beat on the box\nhis hands and smiled and was having a swell time. - after spend\nding ten hours between decks in a situation that must have been at\nleast somewhat like the Black Hole of Calcutta. I thought to myself\n\"Sonny you may live to be a coolie, or you may not live at all, but\njust now you are a man and I take my hat it to you.\" I decided\nwould get to Kowan all right and there would be nothing to it.\nY es terday about two P.M., after giving notice, the Jap-\nanes e started outn to demolish Nantao because there are some Chin-\nese troops there who were cut off and will not surrender. I stood on\nto p of the Dollar Building and watched airplanes drop fifty to ah\nhundred bombs and heard the artillery from near Jessfieldshelling it,\nfiring over\nthe city. It was rather depressing. Nantao is surrounded\non the worth, last, and west by the Whangppoo and the French Concession,\nan a the Japanes e troops have cut off the South side. The French tok\nin all they could, but after all the Nantao is bigger than the Cone e\nession. So Nantao\ntook it. But it isn't my idea of a war. Just cowara-\nly murder. The shelling by the artillery kept up all night at long\nintervals, just to keep the civilians happy I suppose. However to\nforeigners Shanghei is entirely safe and as soon as this Nantao bu-\nsiness is over\n, that will be the end of the fighting in the near viei-\nnity. When our boat passed around Woosung, we estimated there were one\nhundred Japanes e boats,\nmostly transports, anchored in the river.\nUnder other conditions it would have been an inspiring sight.\nI got a message two days ago from Bix in Nanking, saying he was\nleaving for Hankow and for me to meet him there and to go via Hong\nKong, but I decided I could make it this way Quicker, so I am on\nmy way. The result of the present condition of the war will be that\nAmerican boats will soon be calling at Shanghai I think. The Chinese\nsay they are just starting, but I don't know. Will write you fully\nfrom Hankow.\nOn board the s.S.\nChangsha\nI think it is l\nNoT. 14th\nWe made it, but what a trip! That is we got from Nant -\ntungch ow around to Kowan, got aboard another B\u0026s steamer and are\nnow on our way to Nanking.\nThe next morning after the first part of\nthe enclosed letter was written it started drizzling and the weather\nwas awful. We went ashore about ten A.M. as the B\u0026s agent had told\nus no cars were permitted by the military to start out before noon.\nBy ten it was raining and no cars were permitted to go at all, but\nwe learned that a convo y of about eight cars had started about six\nthirty A.M. before the rain started. The damned fool English agent\nwas the dumbest most impossible person I ever met. He couldn't even\nget a sampa n to get us back to the boat for lunch when we found we\ncouldn't get started. The first nigh t we arrived and he came aboard\nhe told us we could get cars the next day at noon provided it diart\nrain. When one of the passengers suggested we ought to get started\nsooner he said, \"If you think you can arrange it better why don't gou\ngo ashore and\ndo it?\" The next morning he came aboard and told us",
